  {/* ==================== 8 · Agents are users ==================== */}
  <Section
    id="agents-are-users"
    step="08"
    eyebrow="Thesis 5"
    title="Agents are users, not a side door"
    lead="Ask in plain language. A change is held for your approval; a table is simply shown, because it was already filtered by your policies before a single row reached the page."
    wide={true}
  >
    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <ScreenshotFigure
        name="agent-proposal.png"
        alt="The helper console showing a proposed administrative change, with the resolved record names and the concrete mutation it would perform."
        caption="The model resolves the request to a concrete mutation, with real record names rather than UUIDs. Authorization is checked before this is rendered — a proposal you could never perform is refused at the point of display."
      />
      <ScreenshotFigure
        name="agent-approved.png"
        alt="The helper console after the human approved the proposal, showing the executed change."
        caption="On approval the action runs — through the policies, into the audit log, with you as the actor. The audit row names the human who approved, not the model that suggested."
      />
    </div>

    <div class="mt-14 border-t border-line pt-10">
      <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">It can also just show you the thing</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 max-w-3xl leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Not every request is a change, and treating one as if it were is how people learn to click
        through confirmations. Asked for a table, the console picks one of the surfaces the
        application already declares — or, when none fits, composes a spec and hands it to the server
        to resolve. A spec is not UI: every resource, field and action name in it is checked against
        a host-configured allowlist by the same verifiers the compile-time DSL runs, so a spec naming
        a field that does not exist is refused with an error rather than rendered blank.
      </p>

      <div class="mt-8 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
        <ScreenshotFigure
          name="agent-surface-legacy-users.png"
          alt="The helper console after asking to see the legacy users, showing a table badged live."
          frame="tall"
          caption="“show me the legacy users” resolves to a declared surface. The live badge is only rendered where the underlying resource actually publishes notifications — a promise the page can keep."
        />
        <ScreenshotFigure
          name="agent-composed-surface.png"
          alt="The helper console showing a table the agent composed, with only the login and email columns, badged composed and live."
          frame="tall"
          caption="“just the login and email columns, sorted by login” matches no declared surface, so one is composed and validated. It is still live: nothing about being designed at runtime stops a table updating itself."
        />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-6 max-w-3xl text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
        Both tables are built by running the resource's own read action with the person asking as the
        actor. What an agent can show you is bounded by what you are allowed to see, not by what the
        agent knows — and that is the same sentence as the one about mutations, which is the point.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:items-start">
      <div>
        <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-pretty">
          An <code class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1.5 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em] text-fg"
            >ash_ai</code
          > tool is not a new code path. It is a <em>declaration that an existing action may be called
            by a model</em> — the same action the admin UI invokes, with the requesting user as the actor,
          through the same policy checks.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">
          If that person cannot assign roles in the UI, the model cannot assign roles on their
          behalf, and nobody had to write an agent-specific rule to make that true. There is no
          agent-specific authorization path — which is precisely why there is no agent-specific
          authorization bug.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The model never holds the mutation. It returns a struct. That is a structural guarantee
          rather than a prompt instruction, which is what makes prompt injection uninteresting here:
          there is no tool to trick it into calling.
        </p>
        <a
          class="mt-6 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-brand underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
          href={withBase('/docs/manifesto/05-agents-are-users/')}
        >
          Thesis 5 — Agents are users <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
      </div>

      <CodePanel code={agentTools} lang="elixir" filename="lib/ash_enterprise/accounts.ex" />
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ==================== 9 · Compliance ==================== */}
  <Section
    id="compliance"
    step="09"
    eyebrow="Thesis 3"
    title="Compliance you inherit"
    lead="Authorization is data: (role, privilege, depth) rows evaluated as a pure union of grants. Three independent paths can authorize a row, and nothing anywhere takes access away."
    tone="muted"
    wide={true}
  >
    <GrantUnion class="mt-2" />

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1.05fr)_minmax(0,1fr)] lg:items-start">
      <CodePanel code={policyUnion} lang="elixir" filename="one clause per grant path" />

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Because every clause is an <code
            class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em] text-fg">authorize_if</code
          >, the sequence <em>is</em> a union and the order cannot matter. A single{' '}
          <code class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em] text-fg">forbid_if</code
          > for row access would break that, so it is prohibited outright — reserved for concerns that
          are not row access at all, like a disabled account or a decommissioned tenant.
        </p>
        <p class="leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Every set those checks read — team ids, the business-unit subtree, the privilege map, the
          reporting chain — is computed <strong class="font-semibold text-fg">once per request</strong
          > into <code class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em] text-fg"
            >ActorContext</code
          >, in a plug, and carried on the actor. A policy check that issues a query is a bug, not a
          slow path.
        </p>

        <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-6">
          <h3 class="text-base font-semibold">The truth table is executable</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
            For every combination of ownership type, privilege, depth and actor placement, the
            conformance suite asserts the expected verdict via <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]"
              >Ash.can?/3</code
            >. It is less a test of the implementation than an executable copy of the specification —
            and the first thing to read when the model surprises you.
          </p>
          <a
            class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 font-mono text-xs text-brand underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
            href={repoHref('test/ash_enterprise/security/conformance_test.exs')}
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener noreferrer"
          >
            test/ash_enterprise/security/conformance_test.exs <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
          </a>
        </div>

        <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Alongside it: an append-only event log with actor and transaction id on every action, a
          correlation id threaded through every effect, attribute multitenancy on every resource, and
          soft delete with cascade. All inherited, none of it per-resource work.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>


  {/* ──────────────── Act III ──────────────── */}
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pt-20 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <div class="border-t border-line pt-10">
      <p class="font-mono text-xs tracking-widest text-brand uppercase">Act III — the evidence</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 max-w-3xl text-2xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-balance sm:text-3xl">
        The part enterprise buyers actually probe, and the part most templates skip
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-4 max-w-2xl leading-relaxed text-pretty text-muted">
        An auditor is not asking whether a control exists. They are asking whether you can show it operated continuously for six to twelve months — which is a question about evidence, and evidence has to be designed in rather than retrofitted the quarter before.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  {/* ==================== 9b · The evidence layer ==================== */}
  <Section
    id="evidence"
    step="10"
    eyebrow="Tamper-evident audit"
    title="An audit trail that survives being tampered with"
    lead="Append-only used to be a property of the application: the resource offered only a read action, so nothing written in Elixir could rewrite history. That is not the question an auditor asks. The question is whether history can be rewritten at all — and until recently, anyone with a psql connection could."
    tone="muted"
    wide={true}
  >
    <Fragment slot="chips">
      <StatusChip status="shipped" label="Tamper-evidence" />
      <StatusChip status="shipped" label="Per-tenant trail" />
      <StatusChip status="shipped" label="Auditor export" />
      <StatusChip status={itemStatus('retention')} label="Retention" />
    </Fragment>

    <div class="grid gap-10 lg:grid-cols-[minmax(0,1fr)_minmax(0,1.05fr)] lg:items-start">
      <div>
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-balance">Two mechanisms, doing different jobs</h3>

        <ul class="mt-5 space-y-4">
          <li class="border-l-2 border-brand/40 pl-4">
            <strong class="font-semibold">The trigger prevents.</strong>
            <span class="text-muted">
              <code class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em]">UPDATE</code> and
              <code class="rounded bg-code-bg px-1 py-0.5 font-mono text-[0.9em]">DELETE</code> on the
              log raise. Total, until someone has enough privilege to lift it — at which point it offers
              nothing.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="border-l-2 border-brand/40 pl-4">
            <strong class="font-semibold">The chain detects.</strong>
            <span class="text-muted">
              Every event carries the SHA-256 of its contents and the previous event's hash. Alter a
              row and every row after it stops adding up; remove one and the link breaks across the
              gap. Dropping the trigger to make an edit does not help, which is the point of doing
              both.</span
            >
          </li>
          <li class="border-l-2 border-brand/40 pl-4">
            <strong class="font-semibold">One chain per tenant.</strong>
            <span class="text-muted">
              Not a compromise — AshEvents already takes a per-tenant advisory lock on every audited
              write, so the serialization a chain needs is already held. A global chain would add
              contention that a per-tenant one does not, and a customer could not verify their own
              trail without being shown everyone else's.</span
            >
          </li>
        </ul>

        <p class="mt-6 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The suite proves it by tampering: it disables each trigger in turn, edits a row, deletes a
          row, forges an event, and asserts each is found. A tamper-evidence claim never tested
          against tampering is not evidence of anything.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        <CodePanel code={auditVerify} lang="bash" filename="the chain, checked" />
        <CodePanel code={auditExport} lang="bash" filename="the sample an auditor asks for" />
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-bg p-5">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold">A customer can read their own trail</h4>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The log is tenant-scoped by the same multitenancy that isolates everything else, not by a
          policy written specially for it. Before, reading it needed a grant over
          <em>every</em> tenant's log — so per-tenant isolation was not narrow, it was absent.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-bg p-5">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold">Impersonation names both people</h4>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          A support engineer acting on a customer's account leaves the customer in
          <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em]">created_by_id</code> and the engineer in
          <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em]">created_on_behalf_by_id</code>. Authorization is
          unchanged: impersonation adds attribution, never reach.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-bg p-5">
        <h4 class="text-sm font-semibold">And what it makes harder</h4>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          A log that refuses <code class="font-mono text-[0.85em]">DELETE</code> collides with a right
          to erasure, and the chain makes it worse. That is now a designed decision — destroy keys, not
          rows — rather than a gap nobody had costed.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mt-10">
      <a class={btnSecondary} href={withBase('/controls/')}>
        Which controls this actually satisfies <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ==================== 10 · Data flow ==================== */}
  <Section
    id="data-flow"
    step="11"
    eyebrow="Where the data comes from"
    title="Ingestion, lineage, catalog, analytics"
    lead="The half this repository is currently silent on, drawn honestly. Each choice is an ADR you can read and disagree with, and each had to clear one bar: can the tool be handed a thin generated declaration and delegate authorization back to ActorContext, or does it bring its own?"
    wide={true}
  >
    <Fragment slot="chips">
      <StatusChip status={itemStatus('ingestion')} label="Ingestion" />
      <StatusChip status={itemStatus('lineage')} label="Lineage" />
      <StatusChip status={itemStatus('catalog')} label="Catalog" />
      <StatusChip status={itemStatus('bi')} label="Analytics" />
    </Fragment>

    <DataFlow
      ingestion={itemStatus('ingestion')}
      lineage={itemStatus('lineage')}
      catalog={itemStatus('catalog')}
      bi={itemStatus('bi')}
    />

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5">
        <h3 class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold">Meltano</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Kept deliberately <em>outside</em> the boundary where authorization applies, so there is no second
          model to synchronise. The cost is stated rather than hidden: staging rows are unauthorized at
          rest.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5">
        <h3 class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold">OpenLineage + Marquez</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The OpenLineage <code class="font-mono text-[0.9em]">runId</code> <em>is</em> the existing correlation
          id. It emits inside the action, after authorization, so lineage records authorized effects rather
          than a privileged crawl.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5">
        <h3 class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold">OpenMetadata</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The least comfortable answer of the set. Its RBAC has Allow/Deny rules where deny wins —
          the exact inverse of a pure union — so no attempt is made to mirror roles into it, and the
          catalogue can never face tenants.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-5">
        <h3 class="font-mono text-sm font-semibold">Superset</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Chosen over Metabase on one fact: Metabase paywalls row-level security even when
          self-hosted, which would put this project's central thesis behind a paywall.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  {/* ==================== 11 · Integration ==================== */}
  <Section
    id="integration"
    step="12"
    eyebrow="Integration hub"
    title="Integrations without M×N"
    lead="One contract per category rather than one per (your app × their API) — surfaced as ordinary Ash actions, so policies gate which actor may trigger a sync and the normal audit log records it."
    tone="muted"
    wide={true}
  >
    <Fragment slot="chips">
      <StatusChip status={itemStatus('integration')} label="Integration hub" />
    </Fragment>

    <IntegrationMesh status={itemStatus('integration')} />

    <div class="mt-12 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-3">
      <div class="lg:col-span-2">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold">The rule that carries the load</h3>
        <p class="mt-3 leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The Nango connection id is <strong class="font-semibold text-fg"
            >never an action argument</strong
          >. It is derived from the actor's tenant. Otherwise the policy engine authorizes the
          operation while the caller picks whose credentials to spend — an authorization check that
          passes on the wrong subject.
        </p>
        <p class="mt-4 leading-relaxed text-muted">
          The residual risk is stated rather than wrapped away: anyone holding the Nango secret key
          bypasses every policy.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-line bg-surface p-6">
        <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold">Verification inverted the design</h3>
        <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-muted">
          Nango's free self-hosted edition covers auth and proxy but gates syncs, actions and
          webhooks. Generating an action per Nango sync would have put the mechanism behind a paid
          tier, so Nango holds the provider edge and the sync logic stays here.
        </p>
        {
          adrHref('0011') && (
            <a
              class="mt-4 inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 text-sm font-medium text-brand underline-offset-4 hover:underline"
              href={adrHref('0011')!}
            >
              ADR 0011 <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
            </a>
          )
        }
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

